WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.680
Good to have you on Planetarian Daily, episode 63, coming to you on May 14th, 2026.

00:00:06.280 --> 00:00:07.820
Here's your science and health briefing.

00:00:08.600 --> 00:00:13.840
Ancient tree rings have preserved evidence of a massive solar storm 800 years ago that

00:00:13.840 --> 00:00:16.020
produced red auroras across the sky.

00:00:16.780 --> 00:00:22.600
Analysis of thousands of modern genomes revealed a previously unrecognized ancestral group linked

00:00:22.600 --> 00:00:25.680
to the ancient emishii people of northeastern Japan.

00:00:26.680 --> 00:00:32.120
The study also traced inherited Neanderthal and Denisovan segments associated with diabetes,

00:00:32.800 --> 00:00:34.280
heart disease, and cancer risk.

00:00:34.740 --> 00:00:40.200
This large-scale effort refines the dual origin model of Japanese population history.

00:00:40.760 --> 00:00:45.980
The same genomic data set could help refine risk models for diabetes, heart disease, and

00:00:45.980 --> 00:00:47.920
cancer in East Asian populations.

00:00:48.760 --> 00:00:54.700
Now, shifting to a very different area of research, Columbia University researchers found that

00:00:54.700 --> 00:01:00.740
rising CO2 levels in the upper atmosphere radiate heat into space more effectively at

00:01:00.740 --> 00:01:02.260
certain infrared wavelengths.

00:01:02.980 --> 00:01:07.940
This mechanism explains why the stratosphere is cooling even as the surface warms.

00:01:08.560 --> 00:01:13.960
Most people assume every increase in atmospheric CO2 simply traps more heat and warms the

00:01:13.960 --> 00:01:14.740
entire planet.

00:01:15.460 --> 00:01:20.260
In the stratosphere, the gas behaves differently by absorbing infrared radiation and then

00:01:20.260 --> 00:01:25.080
radiating that energy outward into space at wavelengths that become more effective as

00:01:25.080 --> 00:01:26.200
concentrations rise.

00:01:26.840 --> 00:01:32.220
Right now, as you breathe, the same molecules that contribute to surface warming are accelerating

00:01:32.220 --> 00:01:34.300
heat loss miles above your head.

00:01:34.840 --> 00:01:40.060
The net result is measurable cooling of the upper atmosphere at roughly half a degree Celsius

00:01:40.060 --> 00:01:41.880
per decade in recent records.

00:01:42.560 --> 00:01:48.120
This separation between layers shows why climate models must track altitude-specific effects

00:01:48.120 --> 00:01:51.360
rather than treating the atmosphere as a single uniform blanket.

00:01:52.040 --> 00:01:57.140
Watch for future satellite data that will refine how these opposing processes interact under

00:01:57.140 --> 00:01:58.040
continued emissions.

00:01:58.900 --> 00:02:04.440
While we map ongoing atmospheric changes, the geological record shows the Sun itself has

00:02:04.440 --> 00:02:06.440
produced sudden, dramatic events.

00:02:07.180 --> 00:02:12.840
Japanese scientists matched spikes in carbon-14 from ancient wood with historical reports of

00:02:12.840 --> 00:02:15.160
red auroras around 1200 CE.

00:02:16.120 --> 00:02:21.300
The data point to unusually short solar cycles and a more active Sun than previously modeled.

00:02:22.060 --> 00:02:27.040
The event offers a rare calibration point for understanding solar behavior and space weather

00:02:27.040 --> 00:02:27.500
risk.

00:02:28.120 --> 00:02:30.800
Solar events leave traces across the solar system.

00:02:31.280 --> 00:02:37.400
Scientists detected iron 60 in ice cores up to 80,000 years old, confirming that the solar

00:02:37.400 --> 00:02:41.260
system is passing through material from a long ago stellar explosion.

00:02:42.260 --> 00:02:47.080
The isotope signature offers a new window into the history of the local interstellar cloud.

00:02:47.960 --> 00:02:52.800
From cosmic debris to regional climate signals, we look at how storms in the southern ocean

00:02:52.800 --> 00:02:54.800
are changing precipitation patterns.

00:02:55.620 --> 00:03:01.100
Measurements from Macquarie Island show that storms now deliver substantially heavier precipitation

00:03:01.100 --> 00:03:02.360
than in past decades.

00:03:03.120 --> 00:03:08.100
The shift suggests the ocean is increasing evaporation rates, a process researchers describe

00:03:08.100 --> 00:03:11.020
as the region's sweating more moisture into the atmosphere.

00:03:11.780 --> 00:03:16.560
The pattern adds to evidence of intensifying hydrological cycles in polar regions.

00:03:17.160 --> 00:03:22.140
From large-scale environmental patterns to the small-scale engineering inside living leaves,

00:03:22.660 --> 00:03:26.480
we examine an unexpected geometric solution in the money plant.

00:03:27.560 --> 00:03:32.940
Researchers mapped the pores and veins in Chinese money plant leaves and identified a natural

00:03:32.940 --> 00:03:38.560
Veronoi diagram, the same spatial arrangement used in city planning and network design.

00:03:39.040 --> 00:03:43.860
The finding shows how plants achieve efficient resource distribution without any measurement

00:03:43.860 --> 00:03:44.360
process.

00:03:45.020 --> 00:03:49.860
The arrangement allows efficient resource distribution without any central measurement process.

00:03:50.420 --> 00:03:55.380
The discovery illustrates how plants solve complex spatial problems through simple growth

00:03:55.380 --> 00:03:55.780
rules.

00:03:56.520 --> 00:03:59.840
Efficiency in living systems also depends on timely partnerships.

00:04:00.560 --> 00:04:05.700
A Frontiers in Microbiology study shows that many young insects face a time-limited game

00:04:05.700 --> 00:04:09.100
of roulette to obtain essential microbial partners.

00:04:09.680 --> 00:04:14.700
Failure to secure these symbionts within the window leads to developmental failure or death.

00:04:15.460 --> 00:04:20.860
The finding highlights the fragility of early-life microbiome acquisition across insect species.

00:04:21.560 --> 00:04:27.120
From terrestrial insects to human spaceflight, we close with a compact device designed to track

00:04:27.120 --> 00:04:29.200
astronaut health on lunar missions.

00:04:29.860 --> 00:04:35.460
A Sao Paulo team developed a compact sensor that NASA will test on the Artemis II crew.

00:04:36.060 --> 00:04:41.540
The device tracks physiological markers tied to radiation exposure and other spaceflight

00:04:41.540 --> 00:04:42.960
stressors in real time.

00:04:43.620 --> 00:04:48.300
Next time, we'll be watching for results from the wristwatch device monitoring astronaut

00:04:48.300 --> 00:04:49.600
health on lunar missions.

00:04:50.200 --> 00:04:52.060
That's Planetarian Daily for Today.

00:04:52.060 --> 00:04:57.860
If you enjoyed this, a rating or review on Apple podcasts or Spotify really helps new listeners

00:04:57.860 --> 00:04:58.580
find the show.

00:04:59.080 --> 00:05:02.660
I'm Patrick and Vancouver, thanks for listening, and I'll see you tomorrow.

